      <div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">00:00</div><div class="transcript-line">I&#39;m leaving this fairly open-ended so you can just I know you guys will just run with it.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">00:07</div><div class="transcript-line">And Clark will go first, I mean on the rank thing and all that.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">00:10</div><div class="transcript-line">That&#39;s however you guys want to do it.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">00:12</div><div class="transcript-line">It just occurred to me.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">00:15</div><div class="transcript-line">Right. Okay.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">00:16</div><div class="transcript-line">Can you give me a minute to put my hearing aids on? I&#39;m sorry.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">00:18</div><div class="transcript-line">No, that&#39;s fine. Go ahead.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">00:23</div><div class="transcript-line">Okay. Well don&#39;t start him yet. Or ask Ray something.</div><div class="timestamp">00:27</div><div class="transcript-line">I&#39;ve heard I&#39;ve heard all his bullshit. Go ahead and get do it. Oh I did I&#39;ll ask no Yeah.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">00:36</div><div class="transcript-line">Guy, if you have something sensitive to say, you want to turn off the thing? We do. Okay, there is Yeah, right. Right, right. Yeah, our our last question on the list here.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">00:50</div><div class="transcript-line">How would we indicate that then, Rich? I mean if we if we did say, just take a real good pause and then say</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">00:58</div><div class="transcript-line">And he&#39;ll shut it off. And then Andrew will write the right. Yeah, it&#39;s it&#39;s good to p pause before you do that though so that we can We can shut shut off the tape and then edit the tape easily. So we if we don&#39;t I mean please you&#39;ll transcribe something. Yes.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">01:17</div><div class="transcript-line">In fact, I&#39;ve got the transcript from our St. Louis for you. Okay. So I&#39;ve got the time.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">01:24</div><div class="transcript-line">Please feel free to you know speak freely. And if there&#39;s some something that you just don&#39;t want on, just let us know and we&#39;ll be very respectful of that and then we can talk about later, you know, the rules that you&#39;d like us to follow with that information. It can always be cut out later too.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">01:37</div><div class="transcript-line">Right.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:40</div><div class="transcript-line">Okay.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">01:40</div><div class="transcript-line">Right.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:42</div><div class="transcript-line">Can&#39;t do that in life.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">01:47</div><div class="transcript-line">We&#39;ll find out if Joe Biden and Taylor.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:49</div><div class="transcript-line">Oh my gosh. No, is it tonight or tomorrow? Tomorrow?</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">01:52</div><div class="transcript-line">Tomorrow. Yeah, they didn&#39;t want to run up against the Cubs today.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:58</div><div class="transcript-line">Can you imagine that? Can you imagine though the pressure that woman is under? I mean Biden&#39;s done this for a living. Yeah.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">02:09</div><div class="transcript-line">Everybody ready</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">02:10</div><div class="transcript-line">Ready, ready?</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">02:13</div><div class="transcript-line">I&#39;m Carrie Tarasuk, the assistant curator of collections, here with Andrew Woods, research research historian at the first Division Museum at Cantini. On Wednesday, October 1st, 2008. We are here with Ray Albin and Clark Welch, both 1st Division veterans who fought at the Battle of Antan.</div><div class="timestamp">02:32</div><div class="transcript-line">The purpose of this oral history is to allow us to ask follow-up questions. Carrie Tarasuk wrote her master&#39;s essay on the Battle of Ontan, and after researching her source her sources, a bibliography which will be attached. There were still some points unclear.</div><div class="timestamp">02:49</div><div class="transcript-line">I&#39;d like to clarify those today. This interview will also be a follow-up to the oral history done with Clark and Ray by Andrew and and carry at the first division reunion in St. Louis in 2007. To get started, Clark, can you please state your rank at the time of the battle, October 17th?</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">03:09</div><div class="transcript-line">I was a lieutenant.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">03:11</div><div class="transcript-line">And Ray, will you also please state your rank at the time of the message?</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">03:14</div><div class="transcript-line">I&#39;m a sergeant at that time.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">03:22</div><div class="transcript-line">Operation Shenandoah 1 and 2, what exactly were their purposes? Can you give us some more information on One and two, please.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">03:32</div><div class="transcript-line">Shenandoah one and two were two operations in the summer and fall of nineteen sixty-seven. They were conducted by the first Inverty Division. with the mission of to search and destroy the two seventy first regiment. The second of the 28th participated in that from 8 October until about 16, uh 17 October when they were taken out of the field. So Shenandoah 1, the second of the 28th did not participate directly in, but Shenandoah 2 they did.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">04:14</div><div class="transcript-line">How do you think Terry Allen Jr. felt about the plan for October 17th? He followed orders, but do you think that he he had a problem with the plan itself?</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">04:27</div><div class="transcript-line">I don&#39;t think he did have a problem with it. I think that uh he was following orders and he expected it to be spectacularly successful.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">04:41</div><div class="transcript-line">Clark, what was going through your mind right after your meeting with Terry Allen the night before?</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">04:47</div><div class="transcript-line">I had two meetings with Terry Allen on the 16th. The first one was in the tent when he gave the briefing to uh the leaders of the battalion as to what was going to happen the next. I think my conversation with him was very short when he put on the board what the plan was for the next day.</div><div class="timestamp">05:09</div><div class="transcript-line">I believe I said something like, what? Jesus Christ, or something like that. Uh I&#39;ve heard other people say I said more than that, but I remember it just saying, whoa, what?</div><div class="timestamp">05:20</div><div class="transcript-line">And then I asked to s visit him in his tent and he said said sure. So after the briefing, he and I, and my radio operator, because I couldn&#39;t see very well in the dark, I&#39;d just be in the lit-up tent, went to his tent and sat and talked for one minute or two minutes And that was where I said, sir, uh, I don&#39;t think I would have said, I don&#39;t think we should be doing this, but sir, we know where they are. We are going to find them right here.</div><div class="timestamp">05:49</div><div class="transcript-line">I hope we&#39;re gonna blow the hell out of them all night. And uh his only response to that to me was uh that&#39;s what we&#39;re gonna do. It&#39;ll be a great day for the Black Lions.</div><div class="timestamp">06:01</div><div class="transcript-line">gonna sneak up on them, it&#39;ll be a great day for the black lions. Those are my conversations with him. I don&#39;t know what he was thinking, of course, but that&#39;s what he said to me.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">06:13</div><div class="transcript-line">What do you think of Newman&#39;s and Coleman&#39;s and Hayes&#39; role in the battle?</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">06:21</div><div class="transcript-line">None. They arrived after the battle was over. Uh I saw General Newman Colonel Newman the night before before the night of the sixteenth, when I was briefing the senior officers on what had happened.</div><div class="timestamp">06:37</div><div class="transcript-line">I don&#39;t remember Colonel Newman saying anything then. Uh General Coleman is where I don&#39;t remember. He did talk to me and he gave me a silver star for that day or before, I&#39;m not sure.</div><div class="timestamp">06:51</div><div class="transcript-line">Colonel Newman? Arrived after the battle was over. Now I can&#39;t say that for sure.</div><div class="timestamp">06:57</div><div class="transcript-line">I can say I did not see Colonel Newman on the day of the battle. I did not see Colonel Newton. General Coleman on the day of the battle.</div><div class="timestamp">07:04</div><div class="transcript-line">I saw no helicopters nearby on the day of the battle.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">07:09</div><div class="transcript-line">And we should clarify that uh Colonel Newman&#39;s the 1st Brigade Commander. Correct.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">07:16</div><div class="transcript-line">General Coleman or is a Colonel Assistant Division Commander. And General Hay was gone. He was in Saigon. So General Coleman was the assistant division commander, but acting as the division commander because General Hay was missing. Gone.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">07:32</div><div class="transcript-line">At what time then? On the 16th or or on the 17th as well?</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">07:35</div><div class="transcript-line">Both days. That wouldn&#39;t be unusual. I I never saw General Hay. I saw him two, three times during the six months I was there.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">07:48</div><div class="transcript-line">Now it was a bit unclear in some of my research, but were there any air attacks before the battle that morning?</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">08:00</div><div class="transcript-line">I am told there were. I have seen it in the logs that there were, but I can tell you I neither heard nor saw anything from the air on the 17th. I did not see or hear any helicopters.</div><div class="timestamp">08:17</div><div class="transcript-line">I did not see or hear any Air Force bombs. Now the Air Force bombs could have been dropped, but they were certainly too far away to either affect me or did not affect the enemy at all in any way. on the 17th.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">08:34</div><div class="transcript-line">And some of us, you know, listen, you know, you listen at night for things and it I heard, you know, we anticipated maybe there&#39;d be airstrikes in the morning. beforehand and we never saw that. And like Clark said, perhaps there were airstrikes, but too far away to be effective for where Delta and Alpha companies were headed. So I never saw or heard any either.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">09:02</div><div class="transcript-line">General Triet, who Colonel Triet, who I met later on. twice in Vietnam. He did not see or hear any B-52s or that&#39;s the word B-52s is their word from hell from the sky.</div><div class="timestamp">09:18</div><div class="transcript-line">He didn&#39;t see or hear them. It was the reason he left at twelve o&#39;clock, he told me, because the B-52s would come. But they certainly didn&#39;t come.</div><div class="timestamp">09:27</div><div class="transcript-line">before I can&#39;t say that I did not see them, I did not hear them. And on the 17th, as on the 16th, our enemy was uh three meters away. That&#39;s from me to you to uh 100 meters away.</div><div class="timestamp">09:42</div><div class="transcript-line">Anything more than 100 meters away, nothing. So if bombs were dropped, they were drop popped a mile away. No no no influence on the battle at all.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">09:58</div><div class="transcript-line">Can you and I I would like you if you could use the um the board to to help and and illustrate but can you please describe the formations of the men on the ground, the enemy included. A Company of course went first and and D next. But if you can help us to understand the perimeter and how everything was sketched out, that would be wonderful.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">10:21</div><div class="transcript-line">Okay.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">10:25</div><div class="transcript-line">And you may want uh it&#39;s up to you, you may want to use red and blue to determine the energy.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">10:30</div><div class="transcript-line">The uh the things that were there. Uh Kam Si River, going that way, and the uh Wangtan River. Going that way.</div><div class="timestamp">10:49</div><div class="transcript-line">Uh our NDP was right about there And the NDP was located so most of it was out in the open so the helicopters some of it was out in the open so the helicopters could land there, and the mortars could fire from there. Ray Albany was the mortar platoon. So within the NDP, Alpha Company, Delta Company, and Bravo Company, and then the headquarters in the middle and would have been my mortars there, somebody else&#39;s mortars there, somebody else&#39;s mortars there.</div><div class="timestamp">11:27</div><div class="transcript-line">Part of it was in the woods. So the woods came along like that And then made that thing called the draw. And then went down like that.</div><div class="timestamp">11:36</div><div class="transcript-line">So that&#39;s woods, jungle. All of this is heavy jungle. I will only try to tell you what I know to be true.</div><div class="timestamp">11:51</div><div class="transcript-line">When it came time for us to leave, Alpha was to lead. So I was, our section of the perimeter was something like that. So I stood there and watched Alpha leave.</div><div class="timestamp">12:06</div><div class="transcript-line">I wasn&#39;t leading. I stood there and watched Alpha lead. And they were in single file, first position.</div><div class="timestamp">12:12</div><div class="transcript-line">Plato, second platoon, third platoon. So uh I know that because I&#39;ve watched them leave. And they headed, I say straight south.</div><div class="timestamp">12:22</div><div class="transcript-line">Uh normally I was very good at navigation. I&#39;d know exactly what I was doing and how far I was going. My job that way did was to follow Alpha Company.</div><div class="timestamp">12:30</div><div class="transcript-line">So although I kept track of it casually, my job was to follow Alpha Company. Company, but generally we went straight south. Of course he came out there.</div><div class="timestamp">12:40</div><div class="transcript-line">He was in single file. So later on, so I got room for Delta Company, the enemy&#39;s down here. Uh he had first platoon, second platoon, uh just give myself more room.</div><div class="timestamp">12:55</div><div class="transcript-line">First platoon, second platoon, third room, in single file. I think he had a total of 60 people. So it&#39;s something less than 20, something less than 20.</div><div class="timestamp">13:07</div><div class="transcript-line">One of them was about 10 men because half of this platoon was left back here I&#39;m not sure which platoon was short. I just watched them all come by. And I think he and his little headquarters are right about there.</div><div class="timestamp">13:21</div><div class="transcript-line">Now behind them, I had my company. I was in the opposite order, 3rd Platoon, 2nd Platoon, 1st Platoon. My first platoon&#39;s job was to stay tucked up behind Alpha Company.</div><div class="timestamp">13:39</div><div class="transcript-line">These are companies Alpha Delta That just shows Alpha first That was my third platoon, and the job was to just, the army would call them connecting files. But his job was just to follow Alpha Company. And his first man was probably six feet behind two meters behind the trail guy in Delta.</div><div class="timestamp">14:06</div><div class="transcript-line">And then I had the second platoon like that in two files. Now tell you why. And then I had the third platoon in two files.</div><div class="timestamp">14:17</div><div class="transcript-line">And that&#39;s because right in here was the battalion TAC. That was a about ten men from the battalion headquarters. The battalion commander, Captain S2, two sergeant majors uh photographer, radio telephone operators.</div><div class="timestamp">14:39</div><div class="transcript-line">But my I had two I had two jobs that day. One was to follow Alpha Company and the other was to protect these guys, the ten people. So that&#39;s what we look like.</div><div class="timestamp">14:52</div><div class="transcript-line">Now every hundred yards or some distance The SOP was that you would stop and send out clover leaves. I don&#39;t know what Alpha did. didn&#39;t see him.</div><div class="timestamp">15:07</div><div class="transcript-line">What we did, and I didn&#39;t expect to find anything because it had already gone through is really just to give, I don&#39;t want to say give the guys something to do, but In case there&#39;s some enemy meet snuck in on us. So every hundred yards or whatever it was, whenever Alpha Company stopped, and my first sergeant had a radio that was tuned to Alpha Company. So I didn&#39;t transmit on that, but I certainly heard everything that Alpha Company said.</div><div class="timestamp">15:34</div><div class="transcript-line">So when Alpha Company stopped and said they were going to send out a clover leaf, I of course would stop and then I&#39;d send out uh Couple men go out and find out if there was anything out there. So I did that. I don&#39;t know what Alfred did.</div><div class="timestamp">15:56</div><div class="transcript-line">They should have done the same thing But that&#39;s what we did. We moved very slowly. We moved about 800 meters in two hours.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">16:03</div><div class="transcript-line">And all that&#39;s being observed as we later found out by the Pabin.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">16:08</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah. That&#39;s where we were. And now I just shouldn&#39;t be in black here.</div><div class="timestamp">16:12</div><div class="transcript-line">That&#39;s that&#39;s Ray Alban and his mortars. That&#39;s Alpha&#39;s mortars. That&#39;s Bravo&#39;s orders.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">16:18</div><div class="transcript-line">You said that</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">16:20</div><div class="transcript-line">Right, right, yes.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">16:22</div><div class="transcript-line">Now he didn&#39;t have a full platoon with him because what I knew is that when we out, when we went out, three or four or five or six or seven or eight of his platoon would come along with us because they wanted to get in it, to get something going. I don&#39;t think Ray came out that day, but I wouldn&#39;t have been surprised if he came out on the 16th when we went down there. So we would have had three or four guys from a mortar plato, but the rest of mortar platoon and some other guys would have stayed back also, two or three people.</div><div class="timestamp">16:54</div><div class="transcript-line">But I ended up with about seventy-five He had about 60. And that&#39;s the way we move. 400 meters per hour.</div><div class="timestamp">17:04</div><div class="transcript-line">Very slowly. Thick, heavy jungle. It would have been called triple canopy, meaning There&#39;s bushes uh six or eight feet high.</div><div class="timestamp">17:14</div><div class="transcript-line">Uh you didn&#39;t have to hack your way through them, sometimes machete. Usually you could just use your rifle, push the stuff out of the way. the way.</div><div class="timestamp">17:21</div><div class="transcript-line">And then there were trees uh 20 feet high, and then there were the big tall jungle trees 100 feet high so that all kind of rained in there continually because water was was dripping down. He&#39;s very slow going. And I had talked to the Alpha Company commander the day before, night before.</div><div class="timestamp">17:42</div><div class="transcript-line">There&#39;s a trail. One trail goes there. There&#39;s another trail that goes across like this.</div><div class="timestamp">17:54</div><div class="transcript-line">And uh when I had made That big contact the day before was right there. And my had made contacts before when he landed on the first NDP, he was down there. So in my mind, the enemy, the enemy was there.</div><div class="timestamp">18:08</div><div class="transcript-line">Enemy was there. I knew where they were. So I had told Alpha Company, you&#39;re gonna find them right there on that trail.</div><div class="timestamp">18:16</div><div class="transcript-line">Be careful. And when I came back to Delta, company. Uh the recon uh the scout the uh mortar platoon leader was there you probably wouldn&#39;t have been there when I called my leaders together.</div><div class="timestamp">18:28</div><div class="transcript-line">I said on my map tomorrow right there is where we&#39;re gonna find them. So bring plenty of ammunition. Then the enemy was doing.</div><div class="timestamp">18:42</div><div class="transcript-line">He had been here for uh three or four or five days. This is based on my conversation with Colonel Triet. I believe Colonel Triet doesn&#39;t know reason for him to make up a story.</div><div class="timestamp">18:53</div><div class="transcript-line">He had come in, crossed Highway 13, and come in here. He was out of food. He was tired.</div><div class="timestamp">19:00</div><div class="transcript-line">He&#39;d been chased by the Americans on the other side of 13. And he had come here to rest, to get rice. We had found all his rice because we are here and the rice came in from Cambodia.</div><div class="timestamp">19:15</div><div class="transcript-line">Had no rice. He hadn&#39;t had any rice for two weeks. As he told me his men were picking leaves off the tree and picking grass off the ground and eating it.</div><div class="timestamp">19:26</div><div class="transcript-line">And uh when they found a place where the rice had been, his men would go through and pick up the rice one grain at a time, and they didn&#39;t eat it. They picked up one grain of rice. And they brought them together.</div><div class="timestamp">19:37</div><div class="transcript-line">They couldn&#39;t build a fire because they were afraid of us seeing the fire. So they just soaked the rice in water and then eat it. As he said, when you do that, you get sick.</div><div class="timestamp">19:47</div><div class="transcript-line">When you get sick, you die, and you don&#39;t ever go home. So he was doing something that normally a regiment wouldn&#39;t do. Normally a regiment would be big and be walking through the you know one up and two back.</div><div class="timestamp">20:01</div><div class="transcript-line">In this case, he had come and he had uh a battalion there and a battalion there. in a battalion. Uh there.</div><div class="timestamp">20:20</div><div class="transcript-line">He was moving this way. He was supposed to join up with the ninth Influent Division over here by uh the mountain Nui Bauden. Uh his regimental headquarters was here He had two, he had his recon company out here.</div><div class="timestamp">20:42</div><div class="transcript-line">They&#39;re the people that I&#39;d found the day before. His recon three platoons were out there because there are open areas out there and he was afraid the Americans were going to land in the open areas. So he&#39;s there.</div><div class="timestamp">20:57</div><div class="transcript-line">And he&#39;s resting, hiding. waiting for rice. And uh we got 142 people doing this.</div><div class="timestamp">21:06</div><div class="transcript-line">I think right now, if we&#39;d uh Put the numbers on there. We had a hundred and I say a hundred and forty-two. Other people say more than that, but we had 142 people.</div><div class="timestamp">21:23</div><div class="transcript-line">Here. We had about uh three hundred people here.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">21:33</div><div class="transcript-line">Later. Right.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">21:34</div><div class="transcript-line">I mean uh now I think.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">21:36</div><div class="transcript-line">Oh. Uh when su when Charlie Casey landed?</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">21:40</div><div class="transcript-line">Uh three hundred, four hundred wrong two hundred. Oh. We had about two hundred people there.</div><div class="timestamp">21:48</div><div class="transcript-line">The enemy head, this is what Colonel Triadhood told me, and it is less than what the intelligence Report said. The intelligence report, which was put out on the 15th, but we never got, said that the 270, this is the 271st Regiment What we called it. Colonel Triet says that&#39;s the secret name they had to fool the Americans.</div><div class="timestamp">22:14</div><div class="transcript-line">What they really were was 1st Regiment of 9th Infantry Division. So down here in red he had 1,200 people. And the intel report said uh 1,500.</div><div class="timestamp">22:29</div><div class="transcript-line">I think he had 1,200 people. Scattered around here because this was a base camp run by Captain Locke. who I met over there.</div><div class="timestamp">22:37</div><div class="transcript-line">Incidentally, Captain Lom&#39;s house is right there. Where Captain Lom lived. And his people were in ones and twos scattered around here because his job was to run this base camp.</div><div class="timestamp">22:58</div><div class="transcript-line">Bring rice in from Cambodia. Kind of keep this place tidied up. So when units like the 271st came through, you could probably had a little hospital there.</div><div class="timestamp">23:12</div><div class="transcript-line">at a clothing place, rice storage, a mechanical room to fix weapons. I saw all of those when I was back there. They&#39;re not there now, they&#39;re just depressing.</div><div class="timestamp">23:23</div><div class="transcript-line">in the ground where they cave caved in. And this is the 8th, 83rd, they had about 200 people. And the The intelligence report said they had about 300 people.</div><div class="timestamp">23:37</div><div class="transcript-line">So I think those are accurate numbers. That&#39;s how things were. This is probably at uh nine o&#39;clock morning.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">23:50</div><div class="transcript-line">Can you show us um just the contact, the point of contact?</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">23:55</div><div class="transcript-line">Oh okay. That that good th thing to do right here. The red guys didn&#39;t move.</div><div class="timestamp">24:03</div><div class="transcript-line">Now what they did do, people talk about snipers being tied in the tree. That&#39;s not true. There were people watching us tied in the trees.</div><div class="timestamp">24:12</div><div class="transcript-line">And he had wires strung through the trees back to the headquarters. This guy, I don&#39;t know if there&#39;s a person there, but he was up here. somewhere.</div><div class="timestamp">24:21</div><div class="transcript-line">He&#39;s in the tree. Sometimes they&#39;re tied in the tree in case they fall asleep. They won&#39;t fall down.</div><div class="timestamp">24:27</div><div class="transcript-line">Sometimes they just wrap some vines around around them. Sometimes they just get up there like a monkey and hang on. But their job is to call back by radio by telephone, not radio because we could hear it radio.</div><div class="timestamp">24:37</div><div class="transcript-line">Telephone line strung in the trees. I&#39;m sure there&#39;s one there. There&#39;s one there.</div><div class="timestamp">24:41</div><div class="transcript-line">They saw us. They knew exactly where we were. None of them moved.</div><div class="timestamp">24:46</div><div class="transcript-line">And we just kept coming south. When I talked to Colonel Triet, and we&#39;re standing right there on a depression in the ground. But caved in.</div><div class="timestamp">24:57</div><div class="transcript-line">He said he prayed to his God that we would turn aside, but we kept coming. And incredibly Incredibly, Alpha Company came down to about here. So that&#39;s Alpha Company now.</div><div class="timestamp">25:16</div><div class="transcript-line">And uh So this is what we look at at about it&#39;s nine forty five I guess. Now we&#39;re uh five meters to my right are bunkers. Now these had been built, and talking to Colonel Triet later, been built in the ground.</div><div class="timestamp">25:47</div><div class="transcript-line">This is the long UN secret zone. They the Americans know that that&#39;s where the head of the Army, the North Vietnamese, or what they call themselves, People Army Vietnam, their headquarters was up here in Campo. Cambodia.</div><div class="timestamp">26:04</div><div class="transcript-line">But where they did all their work was in the Long New Yem secret zone. We knew the enemy was there. That&#39;s a 271st look like that.</div><div class="timestamp">26:14</div><div class="transcript-line">I can give you a better drawing of That&#39;s about 10 o&#39;clock, a little bit before 10. We&#39;re still headed south.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">26:22</div><div class="transcript-line">And the battle began 