over any mortar fire. But as Clark said, said Alpha Company was pretty much decimated within the first ten or fifteen minutes and therefore alpha mortars were out of the picture. And so at some point, and I don&#39;t know what time it was and and I was sitting next to a radio.</div><div class="timestamp">43:21</div><div class="transcript-line">I wasn&#39;t on a radio. I was sitting next to a radio and I had a plotting board here and there was another guy with another plotting board. We always kept two plotting boards to cross-check each other.</div><div class="timestamp">43:30</div><div class="transcript-line">And in in in anticipation of of the companies moving out that morning, we had all our preset locations w with Clark. And I imagine Alpha Company had the same. And I don&#39;t know what Bravo Company were doing because they were out of the loop in terms that They they their company was stationed inside the perimeter.</div><div class="timestamp">43:47</div><div class="transcript-line">They didn&#39;t go on this S D. So in anticipation of the companies moving out, you know, we had the rounds broken down. We didn&#39;t pull off charges on the rounds because we didn&#39;t know the distance, but we knew that the range would be very close, uh probably less than a thousand meters, if there&#39;s going to be any kind of Contact at all. It&#39;d be less than a thousand meters.</div><div class="timestamp">44:08</div><div class="transcript-line">And we also knew that we probably wouldn&#39;t be the first called to fire because Alpha Company was in the was in the lead. And then at the time when it did come to fire, and I don&#39;t remember Clark calling back. I I don&#39;t remember that.</div><div class="timestamp">44:21</div><div class="transcript-line">Maybe I was doing something else. I remember prior to the the um the ambush being sprung that you can hear all the cross traffic on the radios and you know you tune a lot of that out because it&#39;s just chatter chatter chatter and then when the firing started then everybody&#39;s everybody&#39;s aware I mean we were already in the gunholes it&#39;s not like we were you know we were out cutting trees and then had to stop what we were doing and come back everybody&#39;s in the gunholes everybody ready. So when the firing did start, then we said, okay, probably Alpha&#39;s going to get the first shots or whatever.</div><div class="timestamp">44:53</div><div class="transcript-line">So by the time it came for a Delta company to fire, we weren&#39;t firing. And I&#39;m going to I&#39;m just gonna step here for a second. And it would not have been um a a difficult uh a difficult line of fire because we weren&#39;t firing the the the uh Delta Mortars wouldn&#39;t have been firing over the tops of these guys, they&#39;d have been firing to the flanks.</div><div class="timestamp">45:16</div><div class="transcript-line">So we didn&#39;t have to worry about short rounds or rounds dropping on our own guys because it would be uh flanking fire, okay, which was For us it was ideal because you always, and there was a division policy, you don&#39;t want to fire over over the tops of your own guys. So in any event, it would have been a good situation. But I never knew why we didn&#39;t fire until maybe a few years ago, you know.</div><div class="timestamp">45:38</div><div class="transcript-line">And then I I learned that we were prevented from firing. for whatever reason, by somebody higher up than me, and they wouldn&#39;t have been talking to me. They wouldn&#39;t have told me you can&#39;t fire.</div><div class="timestamp">45:49</div><div class="transcript-line">It would have been through the chain like to Lieutenant Mager, the mortar platoon leader. He would have received the instruction from somebody you may not fire. So we&#39;re waiting and waiting and waiting and waiting.</div><div class="timestamp">46:00</div><div class="transcript-line">And you can hear the fire in the distance. It&#39;s only, God, it&#39;s only 400 meters or 500 meters away. And you can, and normally, given all that volume of fire, in the past, you know, firefights, you know, we&#39;ve been right there.</div><div class="timestamp">46:12</div><div class="transcript-line">We&#39;ve we&#39;ve fired. But this it didn&#39;t happen. And I don&#39;t remember Clark, you know, talking on the radio and asking for mortar fire because maybe I was separated at that point, walking around the gun.</div><div class="timestamp">46:25</div><div class="transcript-line">gun hole or something, but I know that we didn&#39;t fire, and I don&#39;t believe there was ever a mortar round fired from that NDP. Now I can&#39;t speak for Bravo Company. I don&#39;t see Bravo Company being in the loop on this.</div><div class="timestamp">46:37</div><div class="transcript-line">I don&#39;t think the Alpha Company ever fired because who was going to call for the fire? I mean they were all dead or people in command and controlled it. And then Delta Company would have been the only one that had the opportunity.</div><div class="timestamp">46:49</div><div class="transcript-line">And I don&#39;t, again, I could be wrong, but I don&#39;t recall us ever, you know, firing around. And again, Clark says we We didn&#39;t, and so I&#39;ll accept that.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">47:00</div><div class="transcript-line">You were a sergeant in the mortar platoon of the case. Yeah, but on fire direction center. Your fire direction center.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">47:09</div><div class="transcript-line">For fire, then there would be a set of coordinates or a pre-plotted point in this case. And then my job was to, that&#39;s why I sat next to the radio. I could hear that.</div><div class="timestamp">47:21</div><div class="transcript-line">And so the radio telephone operator, the RTO, wouldn&#39;t have to relay the information to me. I could hear it firsthand, just like I could hear guys yelling into the handsets and screaming and doing whatever that um I could hear the coordinates or or whatever was happening. And then on my plotting board along with the other guy we would you know plot the points.</div><div class="timestamp">47:41</div><div class="transcript-line">And it wasn&#39;t like we were having to make major corrections. Andrew, it was, I mean, these were minor corrections because we&#39;d been following the the platoons, the patrol, the search and destroy, uh, the company all along. So these were minor corrections that can be could be done that fast.</div><div class="timestamp">47:57</div><div class="transcript-line">And it wasn&#39;t like you&#39;re taking a whole set of coordinates and having to preplot and do all this extra work. So if they&#39;d have been able, if if we&#39;d have been able to fire, it would have been been very quickly, you know, because the rounds were broken down, pull a couple of charges and and hang some rounds and adjust, and that would have been it. But uh didn&#39;t happen Regrettably.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">48:25</div><div class="transcript-line">Can you give us some insight into what was going on? in your mind throughout this battle.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">48:38</div><div class="transcript-line">decided earlier that I wasn&#39;t going to get killed. I had an agreement with God or whatever it is. I wasn&#39;t going to get killed.</div><div class="timestamp">48:44</div><div class="transcript-line">I was going to get hurt bad. Not not in this battle, just whenever I&#39;ve been sent overseas to go somewhere. And I had Done that a couple times before.</div><div class="timestamp">48:52</div><div class="transcript-line">I&#39;d get hurt, but I wouldn&#39;t die. I&#39;d I had a wife and two little boys. A lot of things to do.</div><div class="timestamp">48:57</div><div class="transcript-line">I wouldn&#39;t get so when I got shot, it was really just kind of like god damn I knew that was gonna happen. I don&#39;t want to belittle it, it&#39;s not funny, but when I get shot in the leg, damn. And uh one of the riflemen that happened running by.</div><div class="timestamp">49:14</div><div class="transcript-line">I uh banished my leg. It wasn&#39;t a bad shot, it just uh uh ripped in and out. It was just kind of okay.</div><div class="timestamp">49:20</div><div class="transcript-line">I knew I was gonna get hurt, now I&#39;ve got hurt. I got shot in the back, the chest, Army calls it. Uh damn.</div><div class="timestamp">49:29</div><div class="transcript-line">Now I get bandaged up, but now I have to stay bent over all the time, so it looked kind of stupid. And then when I get shot in the arm, but it was still, I&#39;m not gonna die. I&#39;m not gonna die.</div><div class="timestamp">49:39</div><div class="transcript-line">So with that all taken care of, that wasn&#39;t a thought. The thought was, what am I going to do with my Delta Company? That within, I&#39;ve been in the Army ten years.</div><div class="timestamp">49:52</div><div class="transcript-line">I knew that if you&#39;re in the army you can either shoot or move. Can do both, but it&#39;s not very effective. Unless you got ammo So as I&#39;d done on the 16th, I was ready to, you know, bring a platoon up on the left, bring a platoon up on the right, do whatever the hell the battalion commit plan was.</div><div class="timestamp">50:14</div><div class="transcript-line">But I could not move, partly because of the d fire that was coming in on top of us But mainly because my job was to follow and assist and take care of Alpha Company. And even though when I&#39;d gone forward with Stroop and some of his guys, went with my radio operators a couple times. Uh even though there didn&#39;t seem to be anybody up there, I certainly wasn&#39;t in a position where I&#39;d say, okay, let&#39;s get the hell out of here.</div><div class="timestamp">50:46</div><div class="transcript-line">And then I also had the ten men in the battalion command group. The battalion commander, for Christ&#39;s sake. First time I&#39;d seen him in the field like that.</div><div class="timestamp">50:56</div><div class="transcript-line">The battalion commander, the battalion sergeant. Major, battalion operations sergeant, the battalion S2, three or four or five radio operators, uh Gilbert, the combat photographer. for Christ&#39;s sake.</div><div class="timestamp">51:10</div><div class="transcript-line">But my job was to take care of them and the when I would come by the colonel, I didn&#39;t call him on the radio very much because the colonel was right there. I could just run up to him. and yell at him.</div><div class="timestamp">51:20</div><div class="transcript-line">God damn, sir, what are we doing? Stay right here. Okay, good enough for me.</div><div class="timestamp">51:26</div><div class="transcript-line">Battalion command, the battalion command group&#39;s gonna stay right here. My job is to protect them. I&#39;m gonna stay here and protect them.</div><div class="timestamp">51:32</div><div class="transcript-line">I didn&#39;t do a very good job protecting them. All of them got killed. Not surprising, they got killed.</div><div class="timestamp">51:37</div><div class="transcript-line">They were throwing smoke. They had the big antennas with the ten-foot, they had the big radios with the ten-foot antennas up. But even after they were dead, you don&#39;t go off and leave ten dead guys.</div><div class="timestamp">51:50</div><div class="transcript-line">So my thought was how how am I going to save my Delta? And uh I just kept running around. Not running, if you stood up, he got shot.</div><div class="timestamp">52:03</div><div class="transcript-line">Uh at walking around crawling around. How am I going to get them out of here? How am I going to get them out here?</div><div class="timestamp">52:11</div><div class="transcript-line">And then finally at 1130 when it started to Back not so much shooting at about 1130. I think I did say things like, uh, you know, Stroop, get him out of here, get my Delta out of here. But most of it was just how how am I gonna save them?</div><div class="timestamp">52:34</div><div class="transcript-line">How am I gonna not save them? But what what what can I do? Nobody&#39;s answering.</div><div class="timestamp">52:40</div><div class="transcript-line">So after a while, I don&#39;t know how long I know I talked to Lieutenant Megar, who was the uh his platoon, the really wonderful man, just died a couple years ago. was killed a couple years ago. I know I talked to Lieutenant Megar.</div><div class="timestamp">52:54</div><div class="transcript-line">I don&#39;t remember exactly our conversation, but he the it was screaming and yelling. I can&#39;t I can&#39;t shoot, I can&#39;t shoot. Well god damn it, shoot!</div><div class="timestamp">53:01</div><div class="transcript-line">I can&#39;t shoot, I can&#39;t shoot. Well screw you. And uh I didn&#39;t need the radios to talk to my platoon, except to Meager, and Meager had already told me he couldn&#39;t shoot.</div><div class="timestamp">53:13</div><div class="transcript-line">Well, I don&#39;t want to say I gave up on him. that the rate our radios were getting shot. Anytime I wanted to talk to my platoon leaders, I could yell, Stroop was right there.</div><div class="timestamp">53:25</div><div class="transcript-line">Laberta was right there. My uh middle platoon leader had been evacuated that day. So middle platoon leader was run by a sergeant.</div><div class="timestamp">53:32</div><div class="transcript-line">I just forgot his name, Dwayne Dwayne uh Bert. Dwayne uh Bert. Damn it.</div><div class="timestamp">53:38</div><div class="transcript-line">I don&#39;t, geez. But a good wonderful guy. But I could talk to him.</div><div class="timestamp">53:43</div><div class="transcript-line">You know, Stroop, do this, goddammit. Laberta. The middle guy that was right there at the battalion commander.</div><div class="timestamp">53:50</div><div class="transcript-line">And anytime I wanted to talk, so that&#39;s what you do, you use your radio to talk to the platoon leaders, I could yell at them, grab them. Use your radio to talk to the battalion commander. Battalion commander&#39;s right there.</div><div class="timestamp">53:59</div><div class="transcript-line">Didn&#39;t move an inch. From the when the first round cracked in and got Blackwell. Anytime I wanted to talk to Colonel Allen, I knew where to find him.</div><div class="timestamp">54:10</div><div class="transcript-line">Any radios we hid, we sent up to uh Reddit. the uh where alpha joined uh Delta. That&#39;s where I brought uh Durham.</div><div class="timestamp">54:21</div><div class="transcript-line">That&#39;s where he was killed. And whenever we had enough my radio, I told my radio operator to bring it up, and he stayed up there with Durham for a while. But then that radio would be knocked out, so we ended up with Yeah.</div><div class="timestamp">54:36</div><div class="transcript-line">All of the good radios up there with him.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">54:38</div><div class="transcript-line">And the word platoon, there was no question that like we were gonna be the cavalry and rush out to save them because There was maybe twenty or twenty-two of us or twenty-three of us, and that wasn&#39;t that that wasn&#39;t SOP. I mean that that would have never happened unless somebody&#39;s</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">54:55</div><div class="transcript-line">Under no circumstances would I have to do that.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">54:57</div><div class="transcript-line">Unless Clark would have said, you know, mortar guys come out there, get your gear, and then you know, that&#39;s a whole different thing. Then you&#39;re what you&#39;re abandoning the mortars and you know there&#39;s not much sp I mean there&#39;s some specialization in in the army, but mortars was was specialization in the infantry and a guy couldn&#39;t just come into the mortar pit and and fire a mortar. I mean you could drop around.</div><div class="timestamp">55:18</div><div class="transcript-line">But y you wouldn&#39;t know where it was going. So, and then more specialized was the FDC. There was only two guys that did that.</div><div class="timestamp">55:25</div><div class="transcript-line">And so Well I don&#39;t know if we were smart, but there was if if there&#39;s two f FDC guys and both of those guys leave and then the mortar gun crews leave, then you&#39;re rendering the whole mortar platoon useless. So we stood around just waiting and hoping and figuring the situation would change and somehow we&#39;d start firing like we did on the 16th. And that never happened.</div><div class="timestamp">55:50</div><div class="transcript-line">happened. It it you know like I said before, it never happened. So the our our our hope was that we would be able to fire because we knew that that would help.</div><div class="timestamp">55:59</div><div class="transcript-line">But as far as going out there, that was gonna have to be a rifle platoon and the only rifle platoons available were from Bravo Company, and that was going to be somebody else&#39;s call. And then maybe another company, as it turned out, another company was hella lifted. in there and you know we&#39;re getting ahead of the game now but that&#39;s when there was actually some movement toward the ambush site later on after 12 or 12 o&#39;clock or whenever that start that movement started.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">56:28</div><div class="transcript-line">Whose call would that be at the uh NDP? Jack Sloan. Okay. Yes. Major, right?</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">56:37</div><div class="transcript-line">Major Jack Sloan.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">56:37</div><div class="transcript-line">Excellent.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">56:41</div><div class="transcript-line">There&#39;s two reasons he would have made that call. One reason is when the battalion commander leaves the night defensive position, which he was battalion commander. Commander was down there with us.</div><div class="timestamp">56:50</div><div class="transcript-line">The ranking officer left back at the NDP, who had many more people. Say twice as many people. But there were twice as many people back at the NDP under Jack Sloan&#39;s command than there were forward under the Colonel&#39;s behind.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">57:06</div><div class="transcript-line">And then when Charlie Company joined There was three times as many because that was another hundred guys as well.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">57:12</div><div class="transcript-line">But that was the reinforcement by Charlie Company was later. He&#39;s the senior man in the NDP. Now the other reason that he would have taken it would have been meant his call, his battalion commander was no longer effective.</div><div class="timestamp">57:28</div><div class="transcript-line">The battalion commander was Doing whatever I don&#39;t know what he was doing. I only when I came by to talk to him, he was calling for airstrikes. Didn&#39;t surprise me.</div><div class="timestamp">57:39</div><div class="transcript-line">That&#39;s what he had done on the 16th. And I had said pretty strongly, and I&#39;m told by other people who heard me, that on the 16th I said, if you bring in any airstrikes, I&#39;ll kill you. Because airstrikes happen a mile away.</div><div class="timestamp">57:53</div><div class="transcript-line">And I needed couldn&#39;t even see much further than what you can see in this building. And when I when I talked to uh Colonel Triet two years ago, four years, five years ago. ago.</div><div class="timestamp">58:03</div><div class="transcript-line">Colonel Triet said, we knew the only way we could survive was to grab you by the belt buckle. And he reached out and grabbed me by the belt buckle. That&#39;s the phrase.</div><div class="timestamp">58:14</div><div class="transcript-line">used. So airstrikes coming in a mile away. He didn&#39;t even know about him.</div><div class="timestamp">58:20</div><div class="transcript-line">I didn&#39;t know about them. I wanted the mortars.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">58:27</div><div class="transcript-line">Now according to my readings, um it seemed that there was some there was some question or it it was unusual that Um, Lieutenant Colonel Allen was on the ground with the men that day. Can you tell me a little bit more about that? Was it indeed unusual? What were the feelings? about that?</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">58:46</div><div class="transcript-line">I can answer it real quick.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">58:51</div><div class="transcript-line">You would think it was unusual because you&#39;d never seen him out there.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">58:53</div><div class="transcript-line">I&#39;ve never seen him before</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">58:57</div><div class="transcript-line">Because there were a couple of times when we weren&#39;t in big fights, when we weren&#39;t in the long UN secret zone looking for the goddamn 271st. As a matter of fact, was right there. Uh when for the two weeks prior to that, Colonel Allen never moved with the battle. He stayed back at the NDP.</div><div class="timestamp">59:21</div><div class="transcript-line">I don&#39;t think he was afraid. I don&#39;t think he&#39;s afraid. He just didn&#39;t command that way And on the 16th, he sent some element forward to command Delta Company and Bravo Company, who I never spoke to.</div><div class="timestamp">59:34</div><div class="transcript-line">Well, I did what I caught talked directly with Bravo Company and told Bravo Company, don&#39;t let me Let them come in from our right flank. And so Bravo Company came up on my right flank. And nobody ever did come in there.</div><div class="timestamp">59:47</div><div class="transcript-line">But uh the battalion commander normal Normally stayed back in the battalion as far as I know, always stayed back in the NDP. On the night of the 16th, when I heard them discussing what we&#39;re going to do tomorrow, General Coleman, Colonel Newman, Uh General Coleman, Colonel Newman, Major Hollander, Colonel Newman&#39;s S3, Colonel Allen. And I&#39;m just a lieutenant, so I&#39;m not discussing things, but they asked me what I was doing, and I heard what they were talking about I do not know if anybody ever saying, Colonel Allen, you must walk with the troops.</div><div class="timestamp">01:00:23</div><div class="transcript-line">I was very surprised later on at the briefing when he said, and I&#39;ll be here with you. It&#39;s going to be a great day for the Black Lions. It surprised me that he would be with us when I&#39;m a lieutenant.</div><div class="timestamp">01:00:36</div><div class="transcript-line">He can go anywhere he wants. I thought, man, it&#39;s gonna be a pain in the ass for me to have to have ten big guys who aren&#39;t going to do me any good. They have rifles, but they&#39;re not going to shoot them.</div><div class="timestamp">01:00:49</div><div class="transcript-line">I had never seen him out there with us before. I&#39;d seen him overhead in a helicopter a lot.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:00:54</div><div class="transcript-line">And I was there for seven more months, and I never ever, to my best of my knowledge, saw a battalion commander or any battalion command group walk on on any kind of a patrol or company size or platoon size or reinforced squad size patrol. It just you know that I mean prior to that time I never saw it and subsequent to that time I never saw it. I&#39;m not saying it didn&#39;t happen, but I just didn&#39;t see it.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">01:01:24</div><div class="transcript-line">Would you like to take a break or would you like to keep going?</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:01:27</div><div class="transcript-line">I&#39;m doing all right. I&#39;m doing Okay.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">01:01:34</div><div class="transcript-line">Do you believe that there was an important flaw in the battle plan, or do you think it was a culmination of of errors. Talk to me about that a little bit.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:01:46</div><div class="transcript-line">It was a disaster. I think at one time, I know, the only time I was debriefed after the battle, it was a short debriefing in the hospital, and I&#39;ve seen Captain Cash&#39;s interpretation of that. And I I wasn&#39;t very contributory towards that.</div><div class="timestamp">01:02:10</div><div class="transcript-line">I just said that was screwed up. General DePew, after the after my tour in Vietnam, I came back and became an instructor and a senior instructor in And operations officer for the Florida Ranger Camp. A bunch of really tough guys.</div><div class="timestamp">01:02:26</div><div class="transcript-line">I don&#39;t know how bright we were, but a bunch of really tough guys. I love that assignment. At one time, General DePew, who had grown up from being our division commander, he was now the head of uh trade or something.</div><div class="timestamp">01:02:42</div><div class="transcript-line">He was the head of all forces and all training that required. remained in America. General DePew came down to the Florida Ranger Camp to talk about having a live fire and some other things down there.</div><div class="timestamp">01:02:53</div><div class="transcript-line">And I I was in on that conversation at Brie. strongly. General Cavasso was there.</div><div class="timestamp">01:02:59</div><div class="transcript-line">General Cavasso, who&#39;d been a battalion commander during this battle, we were there. He was about down here. Not coordinated at all, but he was about here.</div><div class="timestamp">01:03:08</div><div class="transcript-line">Insane. General Cavasso was there. Colonel Tucker, who was the camp commander, therefore my good friend, but during this battle he was the S3 for Colonel Cavasso.</div><div class="timestamp">01:03:21</div><div class="transcript-line">became a four-star general. The pew is a four-star general. So five or six of us around the table and went through a debriefing and they said, what happened?</div><div class="timestamp">01:03:29</div><div class="transcript-line">First and only time I was debriefed. I mean it&#39;s happening now, but 30 years went by with not a word spoken. But I did lay out, and at one time General DePew said, what did we do wrong.</div><div class="timestamp">01:03:43</div><div class="transcript-line">And I made a list of 17 things apparently. I don&#39;t have that list anymore, but I had the list of 17 things. Now, it&#39;s a lot of things that are are deadly wrong.</div><div class="timestamp">01:03:55</div><div class="transcript-line">Deadly wrong. Any one of them would have resulted in the Disaster. Now I have at that time the Army hadn&#39;t invented yet the word after action review.</div><div class="timestamp">01:04:10</div><div class="transcript-line">At that time, after a battle, I always talked to as many people as I could. I either did that by going out on the perimeter, which took me a long time because God&#39;s all I would gather the guys together and talk to them. Or sometimes I hit them in the early morning, they would come in to get a cup of coffee, because my beautiful first sergeant would make a big pop.</div><div class="timestamp">01:04:38</div><div class="transcript-line">No, I&#39;d never reached out to the fourth platoon. But I would have a little after and what I&#39;d ask them, I&#39;d get the guys together and try to talk to them all. And generally you want to look at him, you know, make sure he&#39;s all right, because that&#39;s crazy business out there.</div><div class="timestamp">01:04:50</div><div class="transcript-line">And smell the rifle, make sure they fired their rifle. Because I&#39;d heard that people didn&#39;t. Our guys fired their rifles. But I would say, what&#39;d you plan to do?</div><div class="timestamp">01:05:00</div><div class="transcript-line">What happened? What would you do different? And then the next time, who here should get an award?</div><div class="timestamp">01:05:06</div><div class="transcript-line">And then you find out that, uh, well goddamn, it&#39;s Private Jones, that halfway through the battle, Private Jones ran forward and got the only machine gun, brought it back, and started to shoot again. Jones Did you do that? Well, yes, sir.</div><div class="timestamp">01:05:18</div><div class="transcript-line">So I&#39;d write out a little note and send it forward saying Private Jones ought to get a bronze star. I don&#39;t know what happened to him. I think the assholes up there just threw them away.</div><div class="timestamp">01:05:26</div><div class="transcript-line">But we had the little after-action reviews. Now later on, you know, and I was assigned for a while to different places where the after-action review became really important. And we have battlefield operating systems.</div><div class="timestamp">01:05:40</div><div class="transcript-line">And they&#39;ve divided what you do during 