y that he got blamed for for this because he&#39;s the company commander of Delta Company, the Trail Company.</div><div class="timestamp">01:12:13</div><div class="transcript-line">And, you know, there&#39;s got to be something that that went wrong there. But It wasn&#39;t there wasn&#39;t any blame, you know, placed on on Lieutenant Welch.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:12:22</div><div class="transcript-line">And I think that that un unfortunately I&#39;m one of those old fashioned people that From the from when the time I first started school, I wanted to be a soldier. And I&#39;d read somewhere, I want to be a commander. Whether it was a fire table, hell, I wanted to be a fire table.</div><div class="timestamp">01:12:35</div><div class="transcript-line">Have two guys, you know, that&#39;s wonderful. There&#39;s a something I read that a commander is responsible for everything his unit does or fails to do. I still believe that.</div><div class="timestamp">01:12:48</div><div class="transcript-line">I believe that I am responsible for my company. And uh blame, and I don&#39;t want to say that that I&#39;m blamed for everything they do or not do, but I think I was responsible. Anyway, the uh these battlefield systems, I&#39;ll just they&#39;ll go down through them.</div><div class="timestamp">01:13:05</div><div class="transcript-line">Intelligence. Zero. The intelligence we had was, you know, that&#39;s correct.</div><div class="timestamp">01:13:13</div><div class="transcript-line">But it was available. It was not given to us. The second one is maneuver.</div><div class="timestamp">01:13:21</div><div class="transcript-line">I&#39;ll put it on this same map. Our maneuver that day was to do this. Now, because we now know that they had dug in here, here, and there.</div><div class="timestamp">01:13:38</div><div class="transcript-line">They&#39;d been there for five days. They were occupying trenches that had been, he had an engineer