. Complaining Is is commonplace in the Army at the First Division Museum. Any workplace, there&#39;s always complaining.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">01:22:39</div><div class="transcript-line">And blame put it this way, there wasn&#39;t</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:22:45</div><div class="transcript-line">Isn&#39;t any blame placed on the company commander? I don&#39;t think people, I don&#39;t even know if guys that survived, whether they were in the mortar platoon or in the the rifle platoon. platoons.</div><div class="timestamp">01:22:57</div><div class="transcript-line">I don&#39;t even know if they knew that Lieutenant Colonel Allen was out there. I mean the guys that were out there with them did, possibly. So I I don&#39;t know if if I never heard any blame for for anything above you know the the the the company level.</div><div class="timestamp">01:23:15</div><div class="transcript-line">I mean nobody ever I don&#39;t remember everybody ever saying Uh Lieutenant Colonel Allen sure blew that one or something. I don&#39;t even think they knew it just doesn&#39;t filter down that way. You know, I mean Clark would have known and Captain George would have known that Maybe Alan was or Alan was calling the shots, but we didn&#39;t. And so I don&#39;t remember any blame being assessed.</div><div class="timestamp">01:23:36</div><div class="transcript-line">I remember an effort to find out what went wrong. Clark&#39;s successor, the very next day that we got back to life took each of us individually into the mess hall in Lyke and one at a time asked us what we saw, when we saw it, where we saw it, what we did, and what our impressions were. And that was, I understand, from him just talking to him six months ago.</div><div class="timestamp">01:24:00</div><div class="transcript-line">This was Captain Grosso and Captain G-R-O-S-S-O. And he did that upon himself. But he wasn&#39;t doing that to asse