re about ten or twenty feet from me and the two girls girls broke away from me and came running up towards me and the uh girl in white, I forget her name by uh I shouldn&#39;t remember her name. One in red scons will Caroline White came up and said to me, You know who we are? And I said, yes, you&#39;re my colonel&#39;s children.</div><div class="timestamp">01:27:37</div><div class="transcript-line">You are the children of my carnal. And I&#39;ve I physically felt just uh uh whatever it is and far beyond my understand you what but when I was hugging those two girls uh the blame absolutely went away I do believe he&#39;s responsible But the blame went away. And I didn&#39;t want to let them go.</div><div class="timestamp">01:28:00</div><div class="transcript-line">They didn&#39;t want to let me go. They had already read some articles about how I&#39;d said. You know, the colonel&#39;s at blame.</div><div class="timestamp">01:28:08</div><div class="transcript-line">The colonel&#39;s responsible. Everything, I haven&#39;t blamed them. Blamed him.</div><div class="timestamp">01:28:15</div><div class="transcript-line">Actually died when I went back. later on to the place where he died and his uh Consuelo, his daughter, was there with me. Two of us are standing there and I said, it&#39;s right around here, Consuelo, is where he She started crying and said, Daddy, Daddy.</div><div class="timestamp">01:28:33</div><div class="transcript-line">So I don&#39;t blame the colonel. Now, one of my soldiers, and I won&#39;t tell you his name, but one of my soldiers soldiers. Told me a couple of years ago.</div><div class="timestamp">01:28:43</div><div class="transcript-line">See, you know, I hate officers. He was drafted, he&#39;d only stayed in for a couple of years, got out. But generally you&#39;re talking about the bigger.</div><div class="timestamp">01:28:49</div><div class="transcript-line">Very understandable very understandable. He said I hate officers. I said, damn.</div><div class="timestamp">01:28:54</div><div class="transcript-line">He gave his name. I&#39;m an officer. He said, no, no, no, sir.</div><div class="timestamp">01:28:57</div><div class="transcript-line">Not you. I hate officers. He said, I wish Colonel Allenridge had survived so I could kill So there&#39;s at least one man who blames Colonel Allen.</div><div class="timestamp">01:29:13</div><div class="transcript-line">I do blame the system for allowing somebody who was under the pressure that Colonel Allen was under to come back and take command of a battalion in a big fight. I blame the battalion brigade commander for leaving the bodies out there. Blame the division commander for not knowing what the hell was going on.</div><div class="timestamp">01:29:33</div><div class="transcript-line">And accepting a silver star for it after it was over.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:29:39</div><div class="transcript-line">That&#39;s not to say that when I was talking earlier that we didn&#39;t question what happened. Because, and not to be egotistical, but Damn it, this was the Black Lions. I mean, this was a highly successful organization before I got there, and uh they were v we were very good at what we did.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:30:01</div><div class="transcript-line">Couldn&#39;t be happy. When it started, and I&#39;d already told them they see six is going to be sixty. I believe that.</div><div class="timestamp">01:30:10</div><div class="transcript-line">If you see six, it&#39;s sixty there. That&#39;s just the way they are. And later on when the terrible fire just started and kept getting worse and worse, I thought, Jesus Christ, this is more than 60.</div><div class="timestamp">01:30:22</div><div class="transcript-line">More than 10. This can&#39;t be. And then uh when they stopped firing, or when it really quieted down about 1130, and then I&#39;m not sure what happened after that.</div><div class="timestamp">01:30:37</div><div class="transcript-line">I certainly didn&#39;t think, man, we have smacked into the 271st Regiment that the division has been chasing after for four years. Even then I didn&#39;t believe that. When I got at the hospital and then when I got to the hospital in Japan were no contact with anybody even though some of my men were in the same hospital but they didn&#39;t tell me.</div><div class="timestamp">01:30:59</div><div class="transcript-line">It never I never read in the paper that the Black Lions fought the 271st, 1400 of them. Colonel Triette and Captain Blam, when I was standing on the battlefield talking about them. They said all 1,400 of us fired in the Americans that day.</div><div class="timestamp">01:31:16</div><div class="transcript-line">I couldn&#39;t, and that&#39;s in line with what we&#39;re the Black Lions. We&#39;re goddamn good. We&#39;re Delta Company.</div><div class="timestamp">01:31:24</div><div class="transcript-line">We&#39;re about as good as you can get. But man, we really got whack. Must be a couple hundred of them out there.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:31:32</div><div class="transcript-line">So we we did question I&#39;m sorry, were you finished? So we did question, you know, how could that have happened? And then in light of what happened on the sixteenth, when pretty successful operation on the sixteenth.</div><div class="timestamp">01:31:46</div><div class="transcript-line">It was really good. Yes, and and then in light of all that that that most infantry companies have going for them in Vietnam, the helicopter gunship support, uh And prior to the the engagement, you know, we have artillery, we have mortar fire, we have a number of things in our favor. So if any company does get in any kind of trouble you have a number of resources that you can call upon to to make the plane feel level or assert uh a a greater fire superiority or whatever.</div><div class="timestamp">01:32:19</div><div class="transcript-line">So it was this was unthinkable that that that that happened. And not just because we were the black lines, but also but because we had all this support and you always had that support. And airstrikes, you know, that&#39;s always the the given that the that the the pavant could never even uh counter because they had no air force, you know where we were.</div><div class="timestamp">01:32:39</div><div class="transcript-line">So all that. So there was the questioning that went on the days after. And then guys were trying to find out answers.</div><div class="timestamp">01:32:44</div><div class="transcript-line">and you read the Stars and Stripes and you read Time magazine and all these things are are that&#39;s where I learned about reporting that it isn&#39;t always accurate. It it&#39;s to sell newspapers and magazines. magazines, I think, not the stars and stripes, it was free.</div><div class="timestamp">01:32:57</div><div class="transcript-line">But it this wasn&#39;t accurate. And then only years later, you know, did it did the truth, what I guess is the truth, or at least more questions were answered. with the books and being able to discuss things and and this sort of thing too.</div><div class="timestamp">01:33:12</div><div class="transcript-line">This is why this is so valuable.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">01:33:14</div><div class="transcript-line">I gotta show you the the uh American Traveler, the Division newspaper.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:33:18</div><div class="transcript-line">Andrew, I I have that. And I sent that. We did pretty good that to I sent that to Clark after you sent it. I said, I&#39;m not sure if you&#39;d seen this or not, but here goes and and it&#39;s It&#39;s you know, the best that they could do at the time or some you know caveat, you know.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp"