:35:32</div><div class="transcript-line">That was the only action that we had that day Five miles a helicopter in maybe three minutes. Battle started at 10 o&#39;clock. He gets there sometime after 12.</div><div class="timestamp">01:35:43</div><div class="transcript-line">What the hell is going on? And when</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">01:35:49</div><div class="transcript-line">First first he puts uh Sloan in in command and then a few minutes later.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:35:58</div><div class="transcript-line">He owns the NDP. And he owns a battalion because he hasn&#39;t been able to talk to the battalion commander for some period of time. So Sloan is already in command.</div><div class="timestamp">01:36:09</div><div class="transcript-line">Now, now I I am not sure what the answer to your question is. Why would somebody take command? We already had a perfectly good commander standing there who&#39;d been there for a week.</div><div class="timestamp">01:36:27</div><div class="transcript-line">Who&#39;d been there for the battle? A mile away. He never came towards us.</div><div class="timestamp">01:36:32</div><div class="transcript-line">Good question.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">01:36:34</div><div class="transcript-line">And do you know why the battalion executive officer, Major Gillard, was in Lycay that day?</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:36:41</div><div class="transcript-line">Yes, the battalion executive officer should have been in Like. The same well I won&#39;t say the same way. I&#39;d extrane my company, my second in command is my first sergeant, which is kind of against the books.</div><div class="timestamp">01:36:53</div><div class="transcript-line">Your second in command is supposed to be the XO. But I had my exo back at Ly K. His responsibility was to make sure we had food, everything, ammunition, water. He did an excellent job.</div><div class="timestamp">01:37:05</div><div class="transcript-line">Got ice cream through four Four times a week, didn&#39;t we?</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">01:37:07</div><div class="transcript-line">Not a week, but we did get ice cream every once in a while.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:37:11</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah, for that matter. Yeah, so was CXO of AC. Yeah, and he should have been, Tom Grady.</div><div class="timestamp">01:37:17</div><div class="transcript-line">The battalion XO should have been back in Like. Because it we were sending people back to him hurt all the time. He should have been rounding up replacements to send forward.</div><div class="timestamp">01:37:25</div><div class="transcript-line">I only got one replacement on the evening of the 16th. Other than that, it was just constantly whittling down. They went away.</div><div class="timestamp">01:37:32</div><div class="transcript-line">They were shot Nobody&#39;s killed until 17th. So the battalion XO Gillard being in Lyque was perfectly normal and should have been. Now Should he have flown out and taken command?</div><div class="timestamp">01:37:47</div><div class="transcript-line">I don&#39;t know. That&#39;s a you know command call somewhere. But he stayed in Lyke.</div><div class="timestamp">01:37:52</div><div class="transcript-line">That&#39;s normal.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">01:37:53</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah, according to the uh Air Force report that I sent to you Newman had already taken command of the NDP by the time the XO gets back.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:38:08</div><div class="transcript-line">I I don&#39;t know. I I I would guess by w what I&#39;ve heard, but I don&#39;t know.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">01:38:16</div><div class="transcript-line">Uh you&#39;ve read uh Gener General Shelton&#39;s book, The Beast Resign. out there. And uh one of the big questions in that is whether this is an ambush in the terms defined. in uh Shelton&#39;s book or is this a meeting engagement or or what how would you characterize it?</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:38:36</div><div class="transcript-line">That&#39;s one of the slides I may use tonight because it uh now what is it? I know what it is. It&#39;s a search and destroy mission.</div><div class="timestamp">01:38:45</div><div class="transcript-line">That&#39;s one of the missions. Now, it&#39;s a search and destroy. Our job is uh the mission to Sandandoa is search and destroy the two cities 71st. Well, we we we did pretty good in the search part.</div><div class="timestamp">01:38:56</div><div class="transcript-line">We should have found them. But it was a search and destroy. We didn&#39;t do it very well.</div><div class="timestamp">01:39:02</div><div class="transcript-line">Did the search part real good. But it was a search and destroy. Was there an ambush?</div><div class="timestamp">01:39:08</div><div class="transcript-line">Yes. An ambush is an attack, unexpected attack, on a halted or temporary halted enemy. Well, it was an unexpected attack.</div><div class="timestamp">01:39:19</div><div class="transcript-line">We should have expected it, but we didn&#39;t. And we were halted or temporarily halted. It was an ambush. It was also a meeting engagement.</div><div class="timestamp">01:39:31</div><div class="transcript-line">Meeting engagement is a contact between one of the forces is unexpected. unprepared for it. We&#39;re unprepared for it.</div><div class="timestamp">01:39:41</div><div class="transcript-line">We should have had 2,000. Well, usually you want to be about three to one if you&#39;re attacking. So we should have had 3,000 people.</div><div class="timestamp">01:39:47</div><div class="transcript-line">We only had 142 So it was unexpected. That is a meeting engagement. It was all three of those.</div><div class="timestamp">01:39:56</div><div class="transcript-line">It was all three of those. Now there are some other things that it could have been. It was a hasty attack.</div><div class="timestamp">01:40:05</div><div class="transcript-line">We didn&#39;t move very much, but you know, when when you come into fire from the enemy, enemy you conduct a hasty attack. I attacked by fire. So it was a hasty attack.</div><div class="timestamp">01:40:15</div><div class="transcript-line">It was also a movement to contact, sort of. I&#39;m not saying these are the primary things. It was a hasty attack, not a very good hasty attack.</div><div class="timestamp">01:40:24</div><div class="transcript-line">There&#39;s only four or five of us shooting, but it was a hasty attack by fire. It was a deliberate attack. We knew the enemy was there, knew they&#39;d been there for two weeks, knew the day they crossed the river.</div><div class="timestamp">01:40:37</div><div class="transcript-line">So it was a deliberate attack. We just didn&#39;t do it very well. Uh what else was it?</div><div class="timestamp">01:40:44</div><div class="transcript-line">There&#39;s one other thing it was.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">01:40:46</div><div class="transcript-line">There&#39;s a s it&#39;s listed.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:40:48</div><div class="transcript-line">It was a uh movement of contact, deliberate attack, hasty attack.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">01:40:53</div><div class="transcript-line">Surprise. I have I have uh ambush.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">01:40:56</div><div class="transcript-line">It was it uh for sure it was an ambush. It was an ambush. It was a meeting engagement.</div><div class="speake