 are angels.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">02:01:20</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah. Um I read Brigadier General Shelton&#39;s book and uh it was fine. I mean it is it it was interesting to read. I didn&#39;t always agree with some of the conclusions. You know, and he got into a large-scale discussion of five years.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">02:01:39</div><div class="transcript-line">But in the last five years, his book, Shelton&#39;s book, his book and Shelton&#39;s book. have made such a difference. You are angels. Yeah.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">02:01:54</div><div class="transcript-line">Um I read Brigadier General Shelton book and uh it was fine. I mean it is it was it it was interesting to read. I didn&#39;t always agree with some of the conclusions You know, th and he got into a large scale discussion of whether it&#39;s an ambush or this or that and He disagrees with me on what it is, but that&#39;s all right.</div><div class="timestamp">02:02:17</div><div class="transcript-line">And and that&#39;s who? General Shelton? Yeah.</div><div class="timestamp">02:02:19</div><div class="transcript-line">Yes. And that&#39;s okay. You know, it was interesting.</div><div class="timestamp">02:02:21</div><div class="transcript-line">And then when David David Moranis&#39;s book came out, that was very good. And I read the whole thing. I read even the I suffered through the part about the protesters.</div><div class="timestamp">02:02:34</div><div class="transcript-line">And I read that and I I read all the time anyway, so this was it was good. And it was good I I think for everybody that participated. Because they had a chance to talk about it.</div><div class="timestamp">02:02:49</div><div class="transcript-line">For me personally, it was bad, not in the sense of bad writing, but there was a certain element there that I was telling Clark last night at uh after dinner or sometime, that for for many years I put this whole thing I just didn&#39;t think about it. But on October the seventeenth I would be in the classroom, I&#39;d turn my calendar to the seventh And I&#39;d pause a moment and think, or maybe once or twice a year, I&#39;d think about it. But when David&#39;s book came out in 2003 in October Now there hasn&#39;t been a day gone by that I haven&#39;t thought about it at least once.</div><div class="timestamp">02:03:23</div><div class="transcript-line">You know, and whether that&#39;s a good or bad thing, I don&#39;t know. Oh no, no, I I haven&#39;t no I&#39;m not ready to do that yet. And and Clark on conversely tells me that, you know, he&#39;s thought about it ever since.</div><div class="timestamp">02:03:35</div><div class="transcript-line">I mean, since October 18th, 1967, all the way to the present. So I I don&#39;t know how the other guys were. And I I mean it was good writing.</div><div class="timestamp">02:03:45</div><div class="transcript-line">David Moranes is a great writer and he&#39;s a nice guy. And that was a great book. And then came um Robbie Kenner&#39;s uh two days in October.</div><div class="timestamp">02:03:58</div><div class="transcript-line">And I talked to him on the phone for an hour or whatever. And then that one when I watched it, it was captivating because this is, you know, I&#39;m a history, I&#39;m a history teacher and there&#39;s history, and I was a part of that even though I you know wasn&#39;t on the film and you know didn&#39;t use my picture or anything and that&#39;s fine. But there was too much spent on the periphery People on the periphery.</div><div class="timestamp">02:04:27</div><div class="transcript-line">I I can&#39;t understand to this day why a guy, Pinky Durham, who won the Congressional Medal of Honor, is not mentioned in that film. And Colonel Allen&#39;s wife, who might be a nice person, had a tremendous amount of film time. And she was the wife.</div><div class="timestamp">02:04:48</div><div class="transcript-line">of the guy who led us in the battle, so to speak. That did not belong there.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">02:04:53</div><div class="transcript-line">And I should say for the record, and again it Pinky Durham should have had a bigger part in in Maranist. book. Agreed.</div><div class="timestamp">02:05:01</div><div class="transcript-line">But he&#39;s dead. Agreed. You know, and Pinky Durham should have had a bigger part in two days in October.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">02:05:06</div><div class="transcript-line">And I don&#39;t think he&#39;s even mentioned No, no, he he if if they would have said Pinky Durham, that would have doubled the amount of time he had in the in two days in October. I mean he wasn&#39;t even mentioned there. And so with in that regard and and in fairness to Robbie Kenner, he won a tremendous number He won awards for that and acclaim.</div><div class="timestamp">02:05:25</div><div class="transcript-line">And he&#39;s produced a great number of other things for PBS that on war in Guadalcanal. And he told me uh John Brown&#39;s body and I think those are are well done. You know, this one, however, uh, you know, I talked to Rob, he asked me what I thought of it, and I told him I thought it was good, and I didn&#39;t add the part about um Durham.</div><div class="timestamp">02:05:44</div><div class="transcript-line">Then I watched it again and I watched it again and I realized that that should have occurred and there should have been less time spent on the what I call the soap opera aspect. And that&#39;s my opinion, and I&#39;m not in Hollywood, and Robbie is, but And and I don&#39;t know I and then lastly yours which is going to be published and and as I recall the the segments of yours were were right on. You&#39;d taken the time to talk, you cared.</div><div class="timestamp">02:06:14</div><div class="transcript-line">and that&#39;ll be well published. Or it&#39;ll be published and it&#39;ll be done well, I&#39;m sure, when you&#39;re ready. Yes.</div><div class="timestamp">02:06:20</div><div class="transcript-line">And then I also have great fear of This being made into a film. First of all, you&#39;re going to condense it to two hours and twelve and a half minutes or something, being facetious, but they&#39;re going to have to condense it. They&#39;re going to try to sell the film.</div><div class="timestamp">02:06:35</div><div class="transcript-line">And that means that some things are going to change in the interest of historical, you know, accuracy. It&#39;s not going to be there. You know, because they the Hollywood tends to do that.</div><div class="timestamp">02:06:45</div><div class="transcript-line">And if a documentary Can do it two days in October that a film for your entertainment is gonna do it. So I really am concerned about that. And I think that if they ask Clark, I gotta tell you, Clark, I think you should so you can get it help them get it right.</div><div class="timestamp">02:07:01</div><div class="transcript-line">I&#39;m trying to I don&#39;t mean acting. I don&#39;t mean acting in it. I mean somebody there to make sure that they&#39;re gonna at least we have some input to do it right because you you can then say if they don&#39;t do it right, well I was there and I told them this and they didn&#39;t do it, you know.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">02:07:15</div><div class="transcript-line">So you would be like the spokesperson or or I did that for David Moratus. uh I was able to do that. That was that turned out good. I didn&#39;t work for David. I went on my own dime and everything because I could leave if I had to.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">02:07:31</div><div class="transcript-line">But are you talking about the C-SPAN uh Yeah the C SPAN and the C SPAN was there.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">02:07:35</div><div class="transcript-line">The C SPAN Lady made a show called The Writing of a Famous Book by a Famous Author. But but the main thing I did over there was get get to talk to David and to make sure &#39;Cause the the uh Vietnam is communist. They knew what we were doing, they knew our names, they knew that I was a colonel used be a lieutenant.</div><div class="timestamp">02:08:01</div><div class="transcript-line">And they attempted to do some other things. You know, would you like to meet the command of the Fouloi battalion? No. Doesn&#39;t have anything to do with this.</div><div class="timestamp">02:08:11</div><div class="transcript-line">Well, he&#39;s right here, you know, he&#39;s very famous.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">02:08:19</div><div class="transcript-line">I only have Two questions left. Um, and I think you&#39;ve you&#39;ve answered most of one of them, but if you want to add anything, feel free. Um, you know, the popularity of the internet has helped provide and to start and these other sources that we&#39;ve talked about, they&#39;ve helped to provide and start therapy really for many of the survivors who haven&#39;t really talked about it. Um, you know, and reunions have helped as well. I just want to know is there anything that you want to add about, you know, how those things have helped the healing process?</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">02:08:53</div><div class="transcript-line">internet for me was the the the the link that allowed me to be in contact with guys that I hadn&#39;t even and Clark is you know two it sent Since 1967, because the Vietnam War was a strange war. You went over very few guys went over together and came home together. You went individually, except for Sea Packet.</div><div class="timestamp">02:09:15</div><div class="transcript-line">Well those guys didn&#39;t go home because they were killed. But you went over as an individual, you came back basically as an islander, an individual too. So for I mean, I never saw anybody that I was in Vietnam with.</div><div class="timestamp">02:09:28</div><div class="transcript-line">until two thousand and three or two thousand four. And and and that was because the internet started and then um my platoon leader, the guy who&#39;s now deceived Joe Devine contacted me and said, these guys are on the internet and these guys are here. And now there&#39;s not a day that goes by that I&#39;m not in contact.</div><div class="timestamp">02:09:49</div><div class="transcript-line">And I&#39;m not saying it&#39;s always meaningful contact. Sometimes it&#39;s a lot of- It&#39;s easy to delete. Yeah, yeah.</div><div class="timestamp">02:09:55</div><div class="transcript-line">There&#39;s a lot of stuff that just traffic, you know. But the whole point is, like I wrote to those I I you know, one of my philosophical moments years ago. When I first started on this, I said, you know, this these emails are you know quickly deleted, but we should keep doing this as long as our fingers can It doesn&#39;t happen as much as it used to, but once in a while guys will digress and send you something or or ask you something.</div><div class="timestamp">02:10:28</div><div class="transcript-line">And it&#39;s it it brings everybody together so much so that That Jerry Grasso asked six of us from the Delta Company that he took over after Clark left to come to his place in Puerto Vallarta. This is six mortar guys That went down there and just had a great time. And that would have never happened, you know.</div><div class="timestamp">02:10:48</div><div class="transcript-line">And we weren&#39;t always talking about Vietnam and this and that. I mean, you know, that d but we talked about it enough. And th that&#39;s going on.</div><div class="timestamp">02:10:55</div><div class="transcript-line">And it&#39;s not just with with Delta Company, second twenty-eighth. I imagine it&#39;s happening with the first CAV and the and and the twenty-fifth and Infantry division and hundred and first and and name them. You know, it&#39;s happening with all those guys.</div><div class="timestamp">02:11:09</div><div class="transcript-line">So it&#39;s it&#39;s been a a great thing and the guys that care not to participate because they they want to stay the way they work. they you know we get the message and you don&#39;t you know but it&#39;s it&#39;s it&#39;s been a good thing the internet you know for for us you know and for a lot of guys a lot of guys came out of their sh shells. And a lot of guys feel more accepted and gives them a chance to talk.</div><div class="timestamp">02:11:30</div><div class="transcript-line">And even though you&#39;re talking, you know, with keyboard instead of verbal And then guys that I didn&#39;t I never went to a reunion. Then after the internet I I started attending a few and and some I&#39;ll miss, but there&#39;s some that I go to. And I think it came that opportunity.</div><div class="timestamp">02:11:47</div><div class="transcript-line">So it&#39;s been a good thing.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">02:11:49</div><div class="transcript-line">You know, you uh I I&#39;d agree with all of that. You asked if there could be something else. The uh we have the reunions.</div><div class="timestamp">02:11:57</div><div class="transcript-line">Uh we had a reunion here. at at first university museum or this area, whatever this is, this is Chicago, but you were here. It is very helpful, or was very helpful to many of us to come out here and look around at what you have in this museum because what it brought to us is you know that October 17th was a horrid day, a disaster.</div><div class="timestamp">02:12:23</div><div class="transcript-line">Just hard to find anything good. And we left. The next day we left.</div><div class="timestamp">02:12:28</div><div class="transcript-line">Never went back. But if when you come here, you got this group of guys and you come in here, you see that there&#39;s been October 17th all the way back to the Revolutionary Wars. Indian War.</div><div class="timestamp">02:12:43</div><div class="transcript-line">That yes, it was screwed up that day. And yes, I was responsible for that that day. But man, it&#39;s happened a thousand times before.</div><div class="timestamp">02:12:54</div><div class="transcript-line">And I guess sadly it&#39;ll happen again and again. It&#39;s happening today. And it would it was very useful to us.</div><div class="timestamp">02:13:02</div><div class="transcript-line">Reunions are good. You drink, I don&#39;t drink myself, but I I mean I act like I&#39;ve been drinking while I&#39;m there. You drink, you have a nice meal, uh we&#39;re we&#39;re over Las Vegas, we&#39;ll go out and gamble, I guess, next uh later on this month.</div><div class="timestamp">02:13:14</div><div class="transcript-line">Uh but when we came here, we did all of that stuff in Chicago. wasn&#39;t downtown Chicago, wouldn&#39;t it?</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">02:13:19</div><div class="transcript-line">Right. It was the Palmer House. Yeah.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">02:13:21</div><div class="transcript-line">But then we came out and spent some time out here that was the highlight of the thing that uh and and it was the point that uh it was a bad day. But hell there have been bad days all along. Somehow that&#39;s reassuring.</div><div class="timestamp">02:13:36</div><div class="transcript-line">Now that&#39;s that&#39;s crazy, but somehow that&#39;s reassuring. Yeah, yeah, we had a bad day. day.</div><div class="timestamp">02:13:42</div><div class="transcript-line">It happens all the time in our line of work.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">02:13:44</div><div class="transcript-line">And for for me, I mean I this is the first time I&#39;ve been to the museum. I didn&#39;t hook up with the guys that went out here because my phone was dead in the room in the Palmer House. But this the first time I&#39;ve been here and I I mean I&#39;ve always felt like you know I could I&#39;ve read that the black lions were part of the lore and the history of the first division, but then you walk through the exhibits and you see you know the pictures and you just look at the place and you feel even more I don&#39;t know if this is the right word attached I mean to a museum you know can you be attached to but it it was it&#39;s it&#39;s really been a spectacle and that&#39;s part of it too and I wish I would have come here or been able to come here with the guys from Delta Company when we were here in we&#39;re in Chicago in oh five or something.</div><div class="timestamp">02:14:30</div><div class="transcript-line">Oh yeah. Well I my phone was dead in my room. They said they&#39;d call me and the thing was unplugged or whatever.</div><div class="timestamp">02:14:34</div><div class="transcript-line">So anyway, I mean th this is a good thing too. And then you can see the the tradition going back to you know continue to to Operation Torch in North Africa. You can see that Operation Shenandoah has its place.</div><div class="timestamp">02:14:48</div><div class="transcript-line">So you can see that it&#39;s just amazing. And I think that&#39;s a good that&#39;s a good feeling. You know, even though the the topic that we&#39;re talking about, as competitive as I am, I hated to be a loser.</div><div class="timestamp">02:15:01</div><div class="transcript-line">you know and be a part of a losing um thing but that&#39;s the nature of war and then i i i mean i have to tell you that a couple of weeks later the black line Lions did a good job of killing bad guys. And that makes me feel to this very day good. And you know, my wife will not like me to say that, but she&#39;s not gonna see this.</div><div class="timestamp">02:15:24</div><div class="transcript-line">unless I decide to play it and maybe my kids won&#39;t like it either, but they kick some ass in and make me feel goddamn good, you know. And because that&#39;s my thing. I mean that was the way that that that the black lions got back.</div><div class="timestamp">02:15:37</div><div class="transcript-line">And then the rest of the time I was there, we never took many casualties at all, certainly nothing to this extent. And we always dealt out more than we could more than we took. And to me as competitive as I am, I don&#39;t look competitive, but I&#39;m very, and that made me feel good.</div><div class="speaker">Carrie Gabaree (Tarasuk)</div><div class="timestamp">02:15:59</div><div class="transcript-line">For the last question, I want to move back to the battle for just a just a moment. And we understand if you would like to turn the camera off for this question. But We wanted to ask you about how you feel about the decision to have the rest of the bodies um, you know, on the battlefield till the next morning.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">02:16:20</div><div class="transcript-line">Well you know what? I mean it&#39;s uh as far as I&#39;m concerned they could leave it on because to I mean this is Clark&#39;s call too, and if he wants to turn it off it&#39;s fine with me. But as far as I&#39;m concerned, uh you could leave it on because I As a history teacher, this is for perpetuity and I think it needs to be said. Okay, that&#39;s my opinion, but I&#39;m gonna defer to Clark. That&#39;s your call, Clark, because you know more of the big picture than I do.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">02:16:45</div><div class="transcript-line">I I can easily say I wasn&#39;t there.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">02:16:48</div><div class="transcript-line">Oh.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">02:16:49</div><div class="transcript-line">I&#39;m not gonna I&#39;m not gonna say that. Come on man. You gotta help me out.</div><div class="timestamp">02:16:53</div><div class="transcript-line">You can easily say I wasn&#39;t there. So I mean I wasn&#39;t there. So this is a uh a statement of a professional army officer who is a soldier for 31 years and then worked another 12 years working with soldiers.</div><div class="timestamp">02:17:09</div><div class="transcript-line">It&#39;s a incredibly bad decision. When I went back to Vietnam, Captain Lom on the battlefield, stand right there. He came up to me one time and uh as we were talking about said in Vietnamese and he puts his finger there&#39;s real short guys he&#39;s a bad soldier ain&#39;t I&#39;m a bad soldier that you left uh bodies out on the He left the bodies right here.</div><div class="timestamp">02:17:41</div><div class="transcript-line">It wasn&#39;t until five days later that you got the last one, it was right over there. And uh uh that just devastated me. Yeah.</div><div class="timestamp">02:17:52</div><div class="transcript-line">I&#39;m not a bad soldier. But yeah, the people Captain Lom is the only American soldier he&#39;s ever seen the last 10, 20, 40 years. years.</div><div class="timestamp">02:18:02</div><div class="transcript-line">So uh I felt just I can uh Yeah we shouldn&#39;t have done that. Now the other parts of that, we had a full moon that night. I know that.</div><div class="timestamp">02:18:16</div><div class="transcript-line">I&#39;m I&#39;m very aware, not now, but I&#39;m very aware of those kind of things then. We had a full moon. Now cloudy, of course.</div><div class="timestamp">02:18:23</div><div class="transcript-line">It&#39;s cloudy and muggy over there. We had the power and might of the United States Army. We could have run that son of a bitch with napalm so that nobody could have got in and out.</div><div class="timestamp">02:18:34</div><div class="transcript-line">We could have put napalm up and down. We could have got the bodies back. They put 25 bodies in a stack.</div><div class="timestamp">02:18:40</div><div class="transcript-line">That&#39;s what they say And then they fired artillery around them all night. Well, artillery, uh, if you&#39;re not right there watching it to say, oops, that was a little bit long, twenty meters right. 20 meters left, 20 meters right.</div><div class="timestamp">02:18:56</div><div class="transcript-line">Nobody was out there doing that. So those, if you go to the Vietnam Wall. .</div><div class="timestamp">02:19:03</div><div class="transcript-line">And you see the cause of death, most of them are multiple fragmentation wounds. I didn&#39;t see any multiple fragmentation wounds. The guys I saw killed were killed by bullets because there&#39;s somebody there shooting bullets at them.</div><div class="timestamp">02:19:25</div><div class="transcript-line">Well I think uh it was uh I don&#39;t want to say stupid, that&#39;s kind of an emotional word. Bad decision. Now, that battle was over at 12.</div><div class="timestamp">02:19:37</div><div class="transcript-line">12. That&#39;s when the 271st left The only people left then were were caretakers, the guys, and as Captain Lom said, his job is then to run out there and police up the battlefield. One of his jobs, find wounded guys and bring them over to the hospital and find dead guys and bring them over to the he didn&#39;t call them mortuary, he said the dead guys.</div><div class="timestamp">02:20:00</div><div class="transcript-line">So Captain Lom comes running out, and also his best source of fresh water was right at the head of that draw, at the head of the draw. draw. So he he got out and got fresh water for his family.</div><div class="timestamp">02:20:12</div><div class="transcript-line">His family wasn&#39;t living there, but the people that lived in his house. And his job was to pick up bodies. And all those American bodies were there.</div><div class="timestamp">02:20:20</div><div class="transcript-line">And uh uh Carl Horst jumped in there and said uh something in Vietnamese. I said, What&#39;d you ask him? &#39;Cause it uh I resent having a hired interpreter jump in there and try to say anything other than when I say a sentence I want him to say the sentence.</div><div class="timestamp">02:20:33</div><div class="transcript-line">I don&#39;t want him to wait until I said a whole paragraph. So he jumped in there and said, what&#39;d you say? And I I asked him if he&#39;d uh if he&#39;d uh disfigured the bodies in any way.</div><div class="timestamp">02:20:45</div><div class="transcript-line">And I said, well, that&#39;s a hell of a question. I looked right at Captain Lom and said, do you do anything on those bodies? And Captain Lom said, no. And I believed that.</div><div class="timestamp">02:20:54</div><div class="transcript-line">I believe Captain Lamb. Now I am sure that when the 271st, that&#39;s the 1200 people, I think sooner or later, and and they were mixed right in with us, that one of them My guys came to me and said he captured one. What the hell are you talking about?</div><div class="timestamp">02:21:09</div><div class="transcript-line">They captured one right over there. What am I supposed to do with him? Shit.</div><div class="timestamp">02:21:14</div><div class="transcript-line">And I said. I said, let him go. He stood up and ran a couple feet and got shot by the enemy that was boring into us.</div><div class="timestamp">02:21:24</div><div class="transcript-line">But we were we were all mixed in together. And I&#39;m sure that somebody from the two As he ran through us, probably kicked somebody in the head, maybe cut an ear off. What they did.</div><div class="timestamp">02:21:35</div><div class="transcript-line">Sorry. I&#39;m sorry they do. I&#39;m sorry.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">02:21:38</div><div class="transcript-line">Well the books, I mean I don&#39;t I yeah I can&#39;t speak because I wasn&#39;t there, but I read one of the more disturbing parts in the book other than the question that we&#39;re about to answer or answering. Is it they were in among the wounded executing some of the wounded, Clark. And I don&#39;t think it&#39;s a very good thing. I have heard that.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">02:21:55</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah, well that&#39;s why I say I&#39;m sure there&#39;s some people that came through and did that. I&#39;m I&#39;m sure that at least one of my soldiers were shot and killed by the enemy. Sixty-four were shot and killed by the enemy.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">02:22:07</div><div class="transcript-line">But you mean as a wounded?</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">02:22:10</div><div class="transcript-line">As the wounded, yeah. I think that happened. But I believe uh Uh Colonel Triet says he didn&#39;t do it because he&#39;d killed you all.</div><div class="timestamp">02:22:17</div><div class="transcript-line">That&#39;s what he said. We&#39;d killed all the Americans. So we left.</div><div class="timestamp">02:22:21</div><div class="transcript-line">Captain Lom had a job to do. Now, at 12 o&#39;clock, the battle ended. There was no more enemy.</div><div class="timestamp">02:22:30</div><div class="transcript-line">I mean the enemy left. Captain Lau went back to Taking care of his rice and his sick, and his job is to pick up the wounded. And Colonel Triad left.</div><div class="timestamp">02:22:44</div><div class="transcript-line">Now I just forgot that I know. I know exactly when the sun went down that night. Between 12 o&#39;clock and whenever the sun went down, 1800, 1900?</div><div class="timestamp">02:22:55</div><div class="transcript-line">Whatever it was. That&#39;s plenty of time. They could pick up the wounded and carry him back.</div><div class="timestamp">02:22:59</div><div class="transcript-line">You don&#39;t have to carry him back. You only have to carry him about uh two hundred yards and get them to the draw. We could have landed a CHP 47.</div><div class="timestamp">02:23:06</div><div class="transcript-line">It takes 60 people. It takes 80 dead ones. They were spending an incredible decision.</div><div class="timestamp">02:23:13</div><div class="transcript-line">They were spending their time just looking back at it. Incredibly Wrong. Wrong.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">02:23:19</div><div class="transcript-line">Because I recall they were sp &#39;cause I was there then. At that point, we had gotten out there. And they were spending their time, the guys that had the chainsaws, because because it was triple canopy, trying to cut places for helicopters to land as opposed to taking them however many meters it was. to the draw. So but they were having great difficulty with cutting enough space because they could only land the Huey&#39;s and they can only take maybe five or six out at a time.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">02:23:49</div><div class="transcript-line">It could have gone that way.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">02:23:50</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">02:23:51</div><div class="transcript-line">And that&#39;s uh two hundred yards of the draw. And they could have put a CH forty</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">02:23:57</div><div class="transcript-line">And a CH-47 is capable of carry- it could have probably carried all the guys out, right?</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">02:24:03</div><div class="transcript-line">No, they took out some of the dead ones. They took out the ten dead from the command group.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">02:24:07</div><div class="transcript-line">And they took out others too. But the point was, is that is that in reading David Ranis&#39; book, and from what I know, is that it was very difficult to get the choppers into where the evacuation area was. And I think one of the recommendations that I I saw somewhere was that more and better chainsaws need to be to be available post operation or post-battle or at the time or whatever, you know, for you know whatever reason.</div><div class="timestamp">02:24:37</div><div class="transcript-line">Okay? So, you know, that occurred. And that&#39;s a mitigating factor.</div><div class="timestamp">02:24:43</div><div class="transcript-line">But then Clark points out that it&#39;s very difficult. It takes two men to carry a dead guy. I mean, unless you want to carry him on your back.</div><div class="timestamp">02:24:51</div><div class="transcript-line">So We&#39;re carrying guys on stretchers, and it&#39;s difficult, but who cares? You carry them however far it takes and get clear the battlefield. And that didn&#39;t happen.</div><div class="timestamp">02:25:02</div><div class="transcript-line">Fortunately, I didn&#39;t know about them not clearing the battlefield of our dead. until David Moran&#39;s book came out. And I must have read that paragraph, section, chapter, whatever paragraph.</div><div class="timestamp">02:25:20</div><div class="transcript-line">I guess I&#39;ve read those multiple times because I I simply could not believe that. I didn&#39;t know that. But everything else, David Moranis, and I know that the links he went to write factual statements.</div><div class="timestamp">02:25:30</div><div class="transcript-line">So I had to accept that. as the truth. Now I know how many guys, roughly how many guys were carried out, but then his book says that there were 35, 30, 25, 35 remaining.</div><div class="timestamp">02:25:44</div><div class="transcript-line">And nobody gets left behind. I mean, Mike Goodale will tell you in Blackhawk Down, they spent a tremendous amount of money of time trying to evacuate all their killed in 1991 in Mogadishu there. And this is 24 years earlier and the same rule you know occurs.</div><div class="timestamp">02:26:04</div><div class="transcript-line">And And I remember my son and and I think my wife looking at the Blackhawk down, why are they spending so much time and risking so many guys&#39; lives to get dead guys out of there when they can just go get &#39;em back the next day? I said that&#39;s That&#39;s not the point. The point is that if you&#39;re going into combat and you know you&#39;re going to get killed or you know you&#39;re going to get wounded, it&#39;s really reassuring to know you&#39;re not going to get left out there.</div><div class="timestamp">02:26:28</div><div class="transcript-line">And that makes you, you know, all the more uh the more close to the guys that you&#39;re with. That everybody&#39;s in this together and we&#39;re gonna get out of this or, you know, one way or the other, and we&#39;re gonna use either you know come home on our two feet or somebody&#39;s gonna be carrying you, you know. And so for us to leave guys behind, me was un unconscionable.</div><div class="timestamp">02:26:49</div><div class="transcript-line">But I didn&#39;t know about that. But that day, if they would have said, okay, look guys, you guys that are all here, that you know there&#39;s there must have been There was twice as many at least. And Clark probably has the the more accurate figures.</div><div class="timestamp">02:27:09</div><div class="transcript-line">But if they would have said to us, okay, look at we can&#39;t get enough choppers in here, the sun&#39;s going down, we got 25 guys. That we&#39;re not going to be able to evacuate. What do the officers, what do you guys want to do?</div><div class="timestamp">02:27:22</div><div class="transcript-line">I&#39;m sure there would have been many guys stand up and say, well, we&#39;ll just we&#39;ll just spend the night here. and and let &#39;em come and whatever, you know. And whether that&#39;s foolish or I don&#39;t know.</div><div class="timestamp">02:27:31</div><div class="transcript-line">But I know, I mean, it&#39;s easy to say I&#39;m sitting here in a chair, you know, outside of Chicago, but I know what I would have done, you know. And I&#39;m and I know there&#39;s guys there that would have done the same thing. That you, you know, court on all I don&#39;t know if you could have dug in positions or whatever, but something was have been done.</div><div class="timestamp">02:27:48</div><div class="transcript-line">And as far as and like Clark said, if you&#39;re going to adjust artillery on 25 stacked bodies, who&#39;s out there adjusting the artillery saying drop 5-0? or left five zero or whatever. Nobody was out there.</div><div class="timestamp">02:28:00</div><div class="transcript-line">So it was the best guess. And I guess it did work because the next day they went out there, policed not police, they picked up the bodies and and recovered them and they were intact. I think there was two WIA, two MIAs.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">02:28:13</div><div class="transcript-line">Two still missing.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">02:28:15</div><div class="transcript-line">Yeah. But but the point is, is that it it they were left there overnight, and I don&#39;t think that they had to be in a they did have to be, there could have been somebody that stuck with them. And nobody would have been there holding their hands, but there would have been this cordon of guys around there.</div><div class="timestamp">02:28:29</div><div class="transcript-line">And of course, like I said in the we did this in St. Louis. You know, I gotta believe that somebody said that that was probably a bad call because they knew that the the North Vietnamese were coming back or the Pavan were coming back and they were gonna outnumber us and you know we&#39;d all been wiped out there or whatever. But uh uh I&#39;m just saying is that I think that that that had to be done.</div><div class="timestamp">02:28:49</div><div class="transcript-line">You know, but I didn&#39;t know for all those years that those guys were left there.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">02:28:54</div><div class="transcript-line">But as soon as I found that question of were they all dead.</div><div class="speaker">Ray Albin</div><div class="timestamp">02:28:58</div><div class="transcript-line">And some of them apparently they said rigor mortis hadn&#39;t set in on two or three they found the next morning. So therefore, and I think I&#39;ve told you this before therefore those guys must have been alive for a certain amount of time and I I told that to now unconsciousable yeah yeah I I told that to Joe Devine, my platoon leader, in Chicago. We went to breakfast and I said, Joe, I read that book, and they said that Rigor Mortis hadn&#39;t set in on some of those guys.</div><div class="timestamp">02:29:23</div><div class="transcript-line">He said, get over it You know, you can&#39;t go back, you know, but still that bothers me too as much as anything. And I I just think that if guys said known, knowing the type of guys that were in the black lions, knowing the type and any unit. I mean cavalry, you know, like 101st Air, knowing guys, they would have said, we&#39;ll stick around here and see what happens.</div><div class="timestamp">02:29:48</div><div class="transcript-line">You know, give us some more ammunition or whatever.</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">02:29:53</div><div class="transcript-line">But that that brings up a another question. I I heard that the we need to break. Well yeah. Unless you want to have an X-rated I can come back.</div><div class="speaker">Clark Welch</div><div class="timestamp">02:30:06</div><div class="transcript-line">Where do I go?</div><div class="speaker">Andrew Woods</div><div class="timestamp">02:30:07</div><div class="transcript-line">Oh</div>
